model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The runtime adapter replaces the single pool controller instance every few hours whatever the load, and cuts every request in flight at that instant; a replacement must not fail an agent's turn. The model gateway entry treats the platform's reset text or its retryable flag, never an overloaded one, as a controller reset, and resolves the controller again for each send because a stub that threw this error fails again at once. Only an explicit allow-list of model routes is replayable, where a repeat costs at most the tokens of one response: messages, responses, chat and plain completions, embeddings, models and generate-content; a new route is not replayed until it is added. A route that starts a job or makes a paid artifact, such as images, video or speech, is never sent twice, because the provider may already have accepted it. Before the first response byte, a safe read (GET or HEAD without an upgrade, excluding video status polls) or an allow-listed model call without an upgrade is sent once more after one second. A model call's body is read from a tee beside the real send and held, up to 4 MiB, only until the controller answers or the 20-second replay window closes; a larger body is sent once, and the copy giving up mid-body never changes the real send. Held copies in one isolate share a 32 MiB budget, and each reservation expires with its own window even when its request never cleans up. Past the budget a call is forwarded unheld and is not replayable, and one POOL_CONTROLLER_HOLD_SKIPPED line records count 1, request identifier, endpoint template and held bytes. A call whose first send ran past 20 seconds is not sent again, because a device call's signed assertion lives 25 seconds. The pool records each attempt on its own. A job-starting call cut before its answer gets the cautious unknown-outcome answer, so its client decides. A larger body, a late or second reset or a model socket gets the native retry advice: status 503 with an api error for messages and a server error for other model routes, with Retry-After of one second. It is never an overload answer, because the messages client gives up after three of those. A control write keeps its existing failure, because its callers may not assume it was not applied. A started stream is never replayed; a messages or responses event stream ends with the native retry terminal, an api error or a failed response, which the model clients retry by themselves, and a reader that leaves is not an interruption. Other started bodies and model sockets are still cut at this hop and are not recorded there. The shared answer for a model call whose outcome is unknown after dispatch is 502; on an allow-listed route it carries Retry-After of one second and no longer asks the client not to retry, and on every other route, or when the caller names no route, it keeps the cautious do-not-retry answer. Tool calls keep their not-replayed answer. Every failed controller call writes one POOL_CONTROLLER_CALL_FAILED line with the request identifier, endpoint template, attempt, elapsed time, closed message class and the platform's retryable, overloaded, remote and reset flags. Each event writes one POOL_CONTROLLER_INTERRUPTED line with count 1, outcome, saved (a replay the new instance answered successfully), reason, cause, method and endpoint template, and one pool_controller_interrupted observability adapter event joined by request identifier. Exception text, query strings and bodies never enter either."
      constraint "The real-workerd proof of the held copy sends any call whose answer leaves part of its upload unread last: workerd closes that connection, and a later call could start on the closing socket and fail as a client write error rather than as anything the pool did."
      constraint "That call is sent the way a full-duplex client sends it, reading the answer while its upload is still going: workerd answers before it reads the whole body, and a client that surfaces an answer only after its whole upload races the connection closing and fails with a write error the pool never caused."
      constraint "A finite model call whose answer the container's front layer marks resumable survives a controller replacement mid-body: the hop that owns the container marks the call and sends it to the layer's own port only while that container's readiness advertises the layer, and otherwise sends it unmarked to the node's port as before; the controller stamps the runtime that served a marked answer, the entry counts the raw bytes it delivered and re-attaches from that offset through the new instance's resume method, at most three times within twenty seconds, and splices the rest in unchanged. The new instance holds a stream lease for the resumed part, counts it for the code handover and records the call's outcome once under its original id. A failure on the layer's own port costs resumability only: a refused connect sends the call on unmarked to the node's port with a kept copy of a body of known size within the held-copy bounds, whose copy and budget share, like a held copy's, are let go after a 20 s window that outlasts the layer's dial, so a send that never settles holds neither, the layer's answer that the node was unreachable does the same, and any other failure of a running container is recorded as the layer's, so it never quarantines the runtime or starts a recovery. A stopped container, the layer's answer that the node failed, and an unreachable node with no copy to re-send are the runtime's own failures, as on the node's port, and are never sent twice. A resume attempt ends with the twenty-second window, an answer is resumed at most five times and only after its offset moved, and a primary's resume reads readiness without warming the container. A marked answer cut mid-body never counts against its fleet runtime, since a layer exit and a node failure look alike there; it logs one closed line, and the runtime stays judged by readiness and by the layer's pre-header failed answer. The kept copy is sized by the call's Content-Length, which in workerd reaches the hop that owns the container through the entry's and the fleet retry's own copies: on the primary through the controller object, on a fleet runtime also through its object. An answer that is not marked, or that the layer no longer holds, ends exactly as before, with the retry terminal."
      constraint "The platform can replace the pool Worker's isolate under every hop at once, the entry that holds and replays included; only the router outlives it. The router therefore holds and replays too: an allow-listed model call without an upgrade is read from a tee beside its send and held, up to 4 MiB per call and 32 MiB per router isolate, until its answer's headers arrive, and freed after two minutes, because the router has no request signal and a caller who hangs up cancels the handler before its own release runs; a first send slower than that before its headers is not replayed. When the pool binding rejects that send before any answer, the router sends it once more after one second, unless the platform marked the error overloaded, the caller left, the body was not held, or 16 replays already wait or run in that router isolate, so a pool that fails everyone gets at most 16 extra sends per isolate at once; each replay slot expires with the hold window, so a cancelled handler cannot keep one. Past any bound, or when the second send is lost too, the caller gets the unchanged retryable 502. A device call's assertion is signed again for each send, so a replay after a long first send is still live. Every send of one call carries one signed router request id and its attempt number; the controller records the call under that id, so the replay finishes the History row the lost first send started and the caller's X-Request-Id names it, while an unsigned or forged name gives the call its own id. A router-named call whose caller left before its answer records no outcome at the controller: when only the entry went down the router sends it again under the same id, and that send's outcome finishes the row. The router writes one MODEL_FORWARD_FAILED line per rejected send and one MODEL_FORWARD_REPLAY line per call it considered replaying, with request id, attempts, outcome, elapsed time and the error's closed class and platform flags, and the controller writes POOL_ROUTER_REPLAY_RECEIVED for a send past the first. A stream cut after its first byte is not replayed here."
      constraint "The pool Worker runs with enable_request_signal and its compatibility date unchanged, so an incoming request's signal fires when its caller leaves; without request_signal_passthrough a subrequest made from that request, or from a copy of it, is not cancelled by it. Only model traffic follows its caller: at every fetch entry of the Worker, the controller, the operations entrypoint, a fleet runtime and the home peer, any other request is copied without a signal of its own, so its signal never fires and, since RPC refuses a signal the script made, it can cross RPC to the controller, so a fleet command, a start, install, drain or retire, a handover step, a membership change or a migration runs to its end or never starts, as before the flag. A fleet runtime follows its caller only for a model call it forwards to its container's model port. A model call whose caller leaves before its answer stops counting in its runtime at once, while the container finishes it, and the pool records it client_closed when the answer arrives."
      constraint "A runtime's stream lease for a plain HTTP model call names the object instance that took it, because that answer streams through the instance's own code and ends with it; a lease another instance took is over, though nothing of that instance ran its release. A model socket passes through natively and outlives a replaced instance, so its lease names its instance as a socket. A socket lease another instance took, and a lease without a name, which older code or a rollback leaves, end only when the legacy drain's read in the runtime's own container finds no established connection on the model port; the read runs in the background of a count or drain read, only while such a lease exists, at most once every 30 seconds per boot and within five seconds, and a failed, partial or late read ends nothing. A lease the live instance took is never ended by either rule. Each ending writes one POOL_RUNTIME_LEASES_RELEASED line with the row key, kind, count and the connections read, and a read that ends nothing writes POOL_RUNTIME_LEASES_KEPT."
      constraint "The pool Worker's entry and its objects name their isolate with one lazily minted id per isolate. For each model call the entry writes POOL_ENTRY_ISOLATE before its send, with the router's request id, attempt and the entry's isolate, and again on the answer with the controller's isolate, which the controller sets on the answer and the entry strips before any client sees it, and whether the two were one. When the platform replaces an isolate with calls in flight, the calls whose entry carried the controller's isolate are the ones it took down. A call held for the root's recovery or a starting cell ends its hold as soon as its caller leaves, and lets go of the copy of its body it kept."
      constraint "When the pool Worker's isolate goes down mid-answer, the router's binding body does not fail: it ends where the answer stopped. The entry therefore stamps every answer the container's front layer marked resumable with a pool-signed name of its request id, serving runtime and boot, which only the router reads and strips. On a stamped answer the router counts the bytes it delivered and treats a body that fails, or a messages, responses or chat completions event stream that ends before its terminal event (message_stop, a completed, done, failed or incomplete response, an error, [DONE], or a bare error object), as a cut, judged across every resumed part and ignoring events that carry no data, from one list the entry's retry terminal shares; an event too large to hold whose type cannot be read is taken as whole when it closes last, so the router is never stricter than the entry; other streams are cut only by a failed read. The entry passes an answer's own bytes on unchanged and decodes them only to watch, so the bytes the router counts are the layer's. It then asks the pool entry's internal resume route, under its own signed name for the same request id, for the rest from that offset, within the entry's own bounds: one second before each try, three tries inside twenty seconds per cut, five cuts per answer. The route refuses a stamp under any other name and answers a controller it cannot reach yet with a retryable 503, which counts as a failed try; the controller's resume serves the rest from the front layer, which keeps an answer thirty seconds after its reader left. Without a stamp, past the bounds, or when the layer answers gone, the stream ends as it would have. The splice and the stamp load nothing of the pool's runtime, so the router's import graph stays as it was. A model socket the controller passes through natively outlives a replaced controller instance; one it relays for History, as production does, ends with it, and is not resumed."
    }
  }
}
